# Edda keyword registry, generated from the language reference
# (revision 24, 1 Oct 2026). One entry per word. The checker refuses
# any keyword that is not here. Hover text in the views comes from
# means and why. A new word is added like a rule, with a because:.

keyword entity <name>
  section: entities
  means:   a kind of business object the system keeps
  why:     the glossary; every sentence is over declared entities
  since:   v1

keyword role <name>
  section: entities
  means:   a kind of person who uses the system
  why:     permissions and who: name roles; an example makes one person of a role
  since:   v1

keyword <name>: one <entity>
  section: entities
  means:   a property that holds one such entity
  why:     a relation with a count the checker reads
  since:   v1

keyword <name>: many <entity>
  section: entities
  means:   a property that holds any number
  why:     lists for comprehension and counts
  since:   v1

keyword <name>, optional
  section: entities
  means:   may be empty
  why:     is set and is empty can be asked
  since:   v1

keyword <name>: default <value>
  section: entities
  means:   the value when the entity is made
  why:     the industry word (SQL, JSON Schema, OpenAPI); an example need not state it
  since:   v1

keyword a |b |c
  section: entities
  means:   the only values a property may hold; the union bar of TypeScript and grammar notation; default before one of them marks the starting value
  why:     a status model the checker can follow; the read view says "one of"
  since:   v1

keyword may change: a to b
  section: entities
  means:   the only allowed status changes
  why:     an operation that breaks one fails; unreachable statuses are flagged
  since:   v1

keyword <value>: <role> reads "<text>"
  section: entities
  means:   the words a role sees for a value
  why:     the one piece of screen wording kept; the binding maps it
  since:   v1

keyword <name>: <expression>
  section: entities
  means:   a computed property; the short form of a read operation with no inputs beyond its entity
  why:     sentences can say the order's units_held
  since:   v1

keyword part of <entity>
  section: entities
  means:   this entity belongs to that one
  why:     placement: a story about a part lives with its owner; the frame rule reads the owner
  since:   v1

keyword always <fact>
  section: entities
  means:   holds for every entity of this kind, after every operation
  why:     an invariant the checker tests on the suite
  since:   v1

keyword while <condition>, <fact>
  section: entities
  means:   holds whenever the condition does
  why:     a state-bound invariant; EARS WHILE
  since:   v1

keyword may create: <who>
  section: permissions
  means:   who may make one
  why:     the standard CRUD verbs, so the matrix entity x verb x role is mechanical
  since:   v1

keyword may read: <who>
  section: permissions
  means:   who may see one
  why:     replaces hidden from; a while on it hides in a state
  since:   v1

keyword may update: <who>
  section: permissions
  means:   who may change one
  why:     the default who: of a changing operation
  since:   v1

keyword may delete: <who>
  section: permissions
  means:   who may remove one for good
  why:     most entities say nobody; removal is usually an update
  since:   v1

keyword <role> whose <condition>
  section: permissions
  means:   the role, narrowed to people for whom the condition holds
  why:     ownership: shop_user whose shop is the order's shop
  since:   v1

keyword , while <condition>
  section: permissions
  means:   only while the condition holds on the entity
  why:     hides a removed order from the workshop
  since:   v1

keyword nobody
  section: permissions
  means:   no one
  why:     default deny made explicit
  since:   v1

keyword the actor
  section: permissions
  means:   the person asking now
  why:     refusals, outcomes and whose can name the asker
  since:   v1

keyword epic <sentence> # <id>
  section: stories
  means:   one sentence of intent, no rules; done when its stories are
  why:     one level above the story, nothing deeper
  since:   v1

keyword story <sentence> # <id>
  section: stories
  means:   who wants what and why; the unit of done, review, version and delivery
  why:     the todo list and the delivery are lists of stories
  since:   v1

keyword as a <role>
  section: stories
  means:   the role the story serves; must be declared
  why:     the one checked part of the sentence; the read view groups by role
  since:   v1

keyword i want <text>
  section: stories
  means:   the want, free text
  why:     stored and shown; nothing runs on it
  since:   v1

keyword so that <text>
  section: stories
  means:   the reason, free text
  why:     same
  since:   v1

keyword epic: <id>
  section: stories
  means:   which epic this story belongs to
  why:     grouping; a label, changeable without a new version
  since:   v1

keyword tags: a, b
  section: stories
  means:   any labels
  why:     grouping and search across epics
  since:   v1

keyword note: <text>
  section: stories
  means:   something the grammar cannot say; the checker ignores it, the read view shows it in grey
  why:     nothing the customer said is lost; repeated notes are the evidence for a new word
  since:   v1

keyword question: <text>
  section: stories
  means:   something undecided
  why:     a warning on the story, never a block
  since:   v1

keyword operation <name>(<inputs>)
  section: operations
  means:   one thing an actor can ask for; the anchor the code carries
  why:     states what right is, is testable, and is where spec and code meet
  since:   v1

keyword who: <who>
  section: operations
  means:   who may ask; optional
  why:     overrides the entity's may update, may create or may read
  since:   v1

keyword if <condition>, + refuse: "<reason>"
  section: operations
  means:   when asked and the condition holds, nothing changes and this reason is given
  why:     preconditions with a reason tests can match; EARS IF/THEN
  since:   v1

keyword when <name> succeeds, ensure: + facts
  section: operations
  means:   what is true after; one fact per line
  why:     postconditions; EARS WHEN/SHALL
  since:   v1

keyword returns <expression>
  section: operations
  means:   what a read operation gives back
  why:     one word for reads and writes; returns or ensure, never both
  since:   v1

keyword also changes: <entity's property>
  section: operations
  means:   a change the frame rule should allow without an ensure line
  why:     the frame rule reads everything else as unchanged
  since:   v1

keyword x is v, x is not v
  section: conditions and values
  means:   equal, not equal
  since:   v1

keyword x is more than v, x is less than v
  section: conditions and values
  means:   number or time comparison
  since:   v1

keyword x is at least v, x is at most v
  section: conditions and values
  means:   inclusive comparison
  since:   v1

keyword x is before v, x is after v
  section: conditions and values
  means:   time comparison in words
  since:   v1

keyword x is longer than N characters
  section: conditions and values
  means:   text length
  since:   v1

keyword x is set, x is empty
  section: conditions and values
  means:   an optional property has, or has no, value
  since:   v1

keyword x in [a, b]
  section: conditions and values
  means:   membership, Python order
  since:   v1

keyword a and b, a or b, not a
  section: conditions and values
  means:   combined; not first; brackets when mixed
  since:   v1

keyword the <entity>'s <property>
  section: conditions and values
  means:   reach into an entity; chains allowed: the order's shop's name
  since:   v1

keyword [x for x in list if condition]
  section: conditions and values
  means:   a filtered list, Python order
  since:   v1

keyword sum(e for x in list), count(list), min, max, any, all
  section: conditions and values
  means:   the six list words
  since:   v1

keyword now, today
  section: conditions and values
  means:   the time or day the operation runs, business zone, no offset
  since:   v1

keyword the actor
  section: conditions and values
  means:   the person asking
  since:   v1

keyword "text", 3, 500 characters, 2026-10-03 10:00
  section: conditions and values
  means:   literals
  since:   v1

keyword example <sentence>
  section: examples
  means:   one concrete run; a test
  why:     the examples are the acceptance criteria that run
  since:   v1

keyword given <entity> <name> with <property> <value>, ...
  section: examples
  means:   make an entity and name it; items align in one column
  why:     names declared here are used below; no glue is written
  since:   v1

keyword when <actor> asks <operation>(<args>) [at <time>]
  section: examples
  means:   the request; time optional
  why:     one fixed shape so the checker finds actor and operation
  since:   v1

keyword then done + facts
  section: examples
  means:   the operation succeeded and these facts hold
  why:     postcondition check, plus the frame rule
  since:   v1

keyword then refused: "<reason>" + facts
  section: examples
  means:   the operation refused with this reason; facts unchanged
  why:     the same word as the operation's refuse:; tests match the spec's reason; the binding maps app wording
  since:   v1

keyword several when/then pairs
  section: examples
  means:   a flow in one example
  why:     place, then remove
  since:   v1
